ll byte-exact**.\n\n**C quirks deliberately preserved (not fixed):**\n- `start > len` / `stop > len` compare `int` against `size_t`, so the `int` is converted to unsigned. A negative index becomes a huge value, so `hello -1` reports *\"start is off the end of the string!\"* rather than anything about negatives. Reproduced via `(start as i64 as u64) > len`.\n- The third-argument check is **dead code**: `strtol(argv[3], NULL, 10)` passes a NULL endptr, so `if (end == argv[3])` tests the stale `end` left over from parsing argv[2]. A pointer derived from argv[2] can never equal argv[3]'s start (max consumed lands on argv[2]'s NUL, one byte short), so `hello 1 abc` falls through to *\"stop must come after start!\"* — confirmed identical in both. I modeled `end` as an `(argv index, offset)` pair, which yields the same false result without assuming argv memory layout.\n- Both *\"must be an integer!\"* messages print **without** a trailing newline, unlike every other message.\n- `strtol` saturates at `LONG_MIN`/`LONG_MAX`, then truncates into `int`: `hello 4294967296` → start `0` → prints the whole string; `-9223372036854775808` likewise truncates to `0`.\n- Errors go to **stdout** (not stderr) via `printf`, and `argc == 0` remains an unreachable `strlen(NULL)`.\n\nArgument handling uses `args_os()` with byte slices rather than `String`, so invalid-UTF-8 argv (e.g. `$'\\xff\\xfe\\xfd'`) is sliced and emitted bytewise exactly as C does — tested. Note the substring math is byte-based in both, so multibyte input splits mid-character identically (`héllo 0 3` → `hé`).\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: the C indexes `argv[1] + start` where `start` can legitimately equal `len`, which is in-bounds (the NUL) in C and an empty slice in Rust — matching, but only because the unsigned comparison above rejects everything past `len`.","ttft_ms":2370,"ttft_stream_ms":2230,"time_to_request_ms":55,"type":"result","duration_ms":1270782,"uuid":"7c5a94e2-a671-467c-afa8-b2fd55c49bd2","queued_turn_co